e 5, carryover 1.\n\nHundreds place: 6 (first) + 8 (second) +1 (carry) = 15. So 5, carryover 1.\n\nThousands place: 4 (first) + 1 (second) +1 (carry) = 6. Wait, 4 +1 is 5, plus 1 is 6. So 6, no carryover.\n\nTen-thousands place: 2 (first) + 9 (second) = 11. So write 1, carryover 1.\n\nHundred-thousands place: 6 (first) +4 (second) +1 (carry) =11. So write 11.\n\nPutting it all together:\n\nThe digits from right to left are 8 (ones), 5 (tens), 5 (hundreds), 6 (thousands), 1 (ten-thousands), and 11 (hundred-thousands). Wait, so the number would be 1,116,558? Because 11 in the hundred thousands place would be 1,100,000, plus 11 (ten thousands place is 1?), wait let me structure this correctly.\n\nWait, the places:\n\nHundred thousands: 6 +4 +1 (carry?) Let me list each step properly.\n\nLet me write the addition vertically:\n\n        6 2 4 6 7 7\n\n      + 4 9 1 8 8 1\n\n      ----------------\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nOnes: 7+1=8 → 8\n\nTens:7+8=15 → 5, carryover 1\n\nHundreds:6+8=14 +1=15 →5, carryover 1\n\nThousands:4+1=5 +1=6 →6, no carryover\n\nTen-thousands:2+9=11 →1, carryover 1\n\nHundred-thousands:6+4=10 +1=11 →11. Wait, so here, the hundred-thousands place sum is 6+4=10, plus the carried-over 1 gives 11. So the hundred-thousands digit is 1, and we have to carry over the 1 to the millions place. But since there\'s no millions digit, it becomes a new digit. So the total would be 1 (millions), 1 (hundred thousands), 1 (ten thousands?), wait let\'s see:\n\nWait, the places are:\n\nEach digit\'s position:\n\n- The rightmost is ones (position 1)\n\n- Then tens (2), hundreds (3), thousands (4), ten thousands (5), hundred thousands (6), millions (7).\n\nSo when adding the hundred thousands place (position 6):\n\n6 (from first number) +4 (second) +1 (carry from ten-thousands place) =11. So we write down 1 in the hundred thousands place and carryover 1 to the millions place.\n\nThus the total is 1 (millions) followed by 1 (hundred thousands), then the ten thousands digit was 1 (from 2+9=11, so we had written 1 there with a carryover). Wait let\'s go step by step again:\n\nLet me write each digit position step:\n\nPosition 1 (ones): 7+1=8 → 8\n\nPosition 2 (tens):7+8=15 →5, carryover 1\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):6+8=14 +1=15 →5, carryover1\n\nPosition4 (thousands):4 (first number) +1 (second number\'s thousands digit is the third digit from the right? Wait let\'s check the numbers again:\n\nWait, maybe I messed up the digit positions. Let me write the numbers properly with commas for clarity:\n\nFirst number: 624,677 → digits: 6 (hundred thousands), 2 (ten thousands), 4 (thousands), 6 (hundreds), 7 (tens), 7 (ones)\n\nSecond number: 491,881 → digits:4 (hundred thousands), 9 (ten thousands), 1 (thousands), 8 (hundreds), 8 (tens), 1 (ones)\n\nSo when adding:\n\nPosition breakdown:\n\nOnes place: 7 (from first) +1 (from second) =8\n\nTens place:7 (first\'s tens) +8 (second\'s tens) =15 →5, carryover 1\n\nHundreds place:6 (first\'s hundreds) +8 (second\'s hundreds) =14 +1=15 →5, carryover1\n\nThousands place:4 (first\'s thousands) +1 (second\'s thousands) =5 +1 (carryover) =6 →6, no carryover\n\nTen thousands place:2 (first\'s ten thousands) +9 (second\'s ten thousands) =11 →1, carryover1\n\nHundred thousands place:6 (first) +4 (second) =10 + carryover1 =11 →1, carryover1 to millions.\n\nSo the total is:\n\nMillions place: 1\n\nHundred thousands:1\n\nTen thousands:1\n\nThousands:6\n\nHundreds:5\n\nTens:5\n\nOnes:8\n\nPutting it all together: 1,116,558. Which matches the breakdown method. So my initial mistake was in the first calculation where I incorrectly took the second number\'s ones digit as 8 instead of 1. After correcting that, both methods give the same result. So the correct answer should be 1,116,558.\n\nWait, but in the problem, the user might have written the numbers correctly? Let me double-check the original question. The user says "What is the sum of 624677 and 491881?" So yes, 624,677 and 491,881. So the correct sum