nSo when adding:.\n\nStarting from the right (ones place):.\n\nOnes: 8 + 1 = 9. Write down 9.\n\nTens: 4 + 0 = 4. Write 4.\n\nHundreds: 2 + 0 = 2. Write 2.\n\nThousands: 8 (from first number) + 5 (from second) = 13. Hmm, so here we have 13 in the thousands place. Wait, but thousands place can only have a single digit. So we need to carry over. 13 thousands is 1 ten thousand and 3 thousands. So we write down 3 in the thousands place and carry over 1 to the ten thousands place.\n\nTen thousands place: The first number has 0, the second has 4. Adding the carried over 1: 0 + 4 + 1 = 5. So 5 in the ten thousands place.\n\nHundred thousands place: 7 + 2 = 9. No carry-over here.\n\nPutting it all together:.\n\nStarting from the right:.\n\nOnes: 9.\n\nTens:4.\n\nHundreds:2.\n\nThousands:3 (with a carryover of 1 to ten thousands).\n\nTen thousands:5.\n\nHundred thousands:9. So, let me directly conclude the answer. The answer is "]]
