final_output:  ['', "1,165,288. Wait, but according to this breakdown, 1,165,200 plus 78 is 1,165,278? Wait, maybe I messed up the breakdown.\n\nWait, let me do the breakdown again properly:\n\n405,016 = 400,000 + 5,000 + 16.\n\n760,262 = 700,000 + 60,000 + 200 + 60 + 2.\n\nAdding all terms:\n\n400k +700k = 1.1 million.\n\n5k +60k = 65k → total so far 1,165,000.\n\n200 + 0 (from first number's hundreds place) → 200 → total 1,165,200.\n\nThen 16 (from first number's tens and ones) plus 60+2 (from the second number's tens and ones) → 16+62=78. So adding 78 to 1,165,200 gives 1,165,278. Wait, but this contradicts the previous result of 1,165,288. Hmm, so I must have made a mistake somewhere.\n\nWait, let's check the breakdown again. The first number's 16 is in the tens and ones place. The second number's tens place is 6 and ones is 2, so the second number's tens and ones are 60 +2 =62. So adding those gives 16 +62=78. But the hundreds place for the first number is 0, and the second has 200. So that's 200. So 1,165,000 +200=1,165,200. Then adding 78 gives 1,165,278. But in the first method, I had 1,165,288. There's a discrepancy here. Which is right?\n\nWait, maybe I messed up the first method. Let me try adding them digit by digit again:\n\nLet's write them vertically:\n\n\u202f405016\n\n+760262\n\n------------\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nOnes: 6+2=8\n\nTens:1+6=7\n\nHundreds:0+2=2\n\nThousands:5+0=5\n\nTen thousands:0+6=6\n\nHundred thousands:4+7=11. So here, 11, so we write 1 in the hundred thousands place and carry over 1 to the millions place. So the millions place is 1 (from the carry). So putting it all together:\n\nMillions:1\n\nHundred thousands:1 (from the 11) ?\n\nWait, hold on, maybe my first calculation was wrong here. Let me detail each digit's place properly.\n\nLet me write the numbers with their place values:\n\nFirst number: 405,016\n\nDigits from right (ones to hundred thousands):\n\nPosition: 1 (ones):6\n\n2 (tens):1\n\n3 (hundreds):0\n\n4 (thousa