+9=15 +1=16. So 6 in hundreds, carryover1. Correct.\n\nThousands place: first number\'s thousands digit is 6, second\'s thousands digit is 9. Wait, yes. 6 (from first) +9 (second) =15, plus the carryover 1 is 16. So thousands place: 6, carryover1 again?\n\nWait, hold on, this is getting confusing. Let me write each digit\'s place properly.\n\nLet me list each digit position by position:\n\nNumber1: 3 3 6 6 9 5\n\nNumber2: 3 4 9 9 5 3\n\nWait, perhaps writing them as:\n\nLet me align the digits properly:\n\n        3 3 6 6 9 5\n\n      + 3 4 9 9 5 3\n\n      ----------------\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (ones place):\n\nOnes: 5 +3 =8.\n\nTens: 9 +5=14 (write 4, carryover1)\n\nHundreds:6 +9=15 +1=16 (write6, carryover1)\n\nThousands:6 (from first number\'s thousands place) +9 (from second\'s thousands) =15 +1=16 (write6, carryover1)\n\nTen-thousands:3 (first\'s ten-thousands) +4 (second\'s) =7 +1=8 (wait, carryover from thousands place was 1, so 3+4 +1=8. So write8, no carryover?)\n\nWait, let\'s see:\n\nWait the ten-thousands place:\n\nFirst number\'s ten thousands digit is 3 (since it\'s 3 (hundred thousands), 3 (ten thousands), 6 (thousands), etc.)\n\nSecond number\'s ten thousands digit is 4.\n\nSo adding those: 3 +4 =7, plus any carryover from the thousands place. The thousands place addition was 6 +9 =15 plus the previous carryover 1 gives 16. So the thousands digit is 6, carryover 1 to ten thousands.\n\nThus, ten thousands place: 3+4 +1 (carryover) = 8. So no carryover here.\n\nThen hundred thousands place: 3 (from first) +3 (second) =6. Plus no carryover, so total 6.\n\nSo putting it all together:\n\nThe sum would be:\n\nHundred thousands:6\n\nTen thousands:8\n\nThousands:6\n\nHundreds:6\n\nTens:4\n\nOnes:8\n\nWait, so that would be 686,648?\n\nWait, but let me check again step by step:\n\nLet me write the addition vertically:\n\n      3 3 6 6 9 5\n\n    + 3 4 9 9 5 3\n\n    ----------------\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nOnes:5+3=8.\n\nTens:9+5=14 → 4