, hold on, maybe I messed up the places.\n\nWait, let me check again. Let me write out each digit's place clearly.\n\nBreaking down both numbers:.\n\n930,975:.\n- Hundred thousands: 9.\n- Ten thousands: 3.\n- Thousands: 0.\n- Hundreds: 9.\n- Tens: 7.\n- Ones: 5.\n\n313,746:.\n- Hundred thousands: 3.\n- Ten thousands: 1.\n- Thousands: 3.\n- Hundreds: 7.\n- Tens: 4.\n- Ones: 6.\n\nAdding each column:.\n\nStarting from the right (ones):.\n\nOnes: 5 + 6 = 11 → 1, carryover 1.\nTens: 7 + 4 = 11 + 1 = 12 → 2, carryover 1.\nHundreds: 9 + 7 = 16 + 1 = 17 → 7, carryover 1.\nThousands: 0 + 3 = 3 + 1 = 4 → 4, no carryover.\nTen thousands: 3 + 1 = 4 → 4.\nHundred thousands: 9 + 3 = 12 → 12. Wait, that's 12 in the hundred thousands place, which would mean 1,200,000. But how do we write that? Since it's two digits, we need to carry over the 1 to the millions place. Oh right, I almost forgot that. So 9 (hundred thousands) + 3 (hundred thousands) = 12, so that's 2 in the hundred thousands and carryover 1 to the millions place. Since there were no millions in the original numbers, the carryover becomes 1 in the millions place.\n\nSo putting it all together:.\n\nMillions place: 1.\nHundred thousands: 2.\nTen thousands: 4.\nThousands: 4.\nHundreds: 7.\nTens: 2.\nOnes: 1. So, let me directly conclude the answer. The answer is "]]
