t 2 in the thousands place and carry over another 1 to the next place (ten thousands).\n\nNext, the ten thousands place. The first number has 5, the second has 4. 5 + 4 = 9. Adding the carried over 1 gives 10. So we write 0 in the ten thousands place and carry over 1 again to the hundred thousands place.\n\nFinally, the hundred thousands place. Both numbers have 8 each. 8 + 8 = 16. Plus the carried over 1 makes it 17. So that's 7 in the hundred thousands place and a carried over 1 to the millions place. Since there are no more digits, that carried over 1 becomes a 1 in the millions place.\n\nPutting it all together, starting from the millions place down: 1 (million), 7 (hundred thousands), 0 (ten thousands), 2 (thousands), 2 (hundreds), 6 (tens), and 9 (ones). Wait, let me check that again. Wait, the places might be getting mixed up here.\n\nWait, actually, let me recount each digit's position step by step to make sure I didn't mix up any places. Let me write it out step by step again carefully.\n\nLet me align the numbers properly:.\n\n       8 5 6 5 3 1.\n     + 8 4 5 7 3 8.\n     ----------------.\nStarting from the right (ones place):.\n\nOnes: 1 + 8 = 9. Write down 9.\n\nTens: 3 + 3 = 6. Write down 6.\n\nHundreds: 5 + 7 = 12. Write down 2, carry over 1.\n\nThousands: 6 + 5 = 11, plus the carried 1 is 12. Write 2, carry over 1.\n\nTen thousands: 5 + 4 = 9, plus the carried 1 is 10. Write 0, carry over 1.\n\nHundred thousands: 8 + 8 = 16, plus the carried 1 is 17. So we write 7 here and carry over 1 to the millions place.\n\nSince there are no more digits, the carried 1 becomes the millions digit. So, let me directly conclude the answer. The answer is "]]
