So we need to write down 1 in the hundred-thousands place and carry over 1 to the millions place. But since there are no higher digits, that carried-over 1 becomes a new digit at the beginning. .\n\nPutting it all together, starting from the right:.\n\nOnes: 5.\n\nTens: 2.\n\nHundreds: 5.\n\nThousands: 9.\n\nTen-thousands: 5.\n\nHundred-thousands: 1 (with a carryover of 1).\n\nThen the millions place: the carried-over 1.\n\nSo combining all these digits: 1 (million), 1 (hundred-thousand), 5 (ten-thousand), 9 (thousand), 5 (hundred), 2 (ten), 5 (one). Wait, let me check again.\n\nWait, maybe I messed up the places. Let me recount:.\n\nWait, let's do this step by step again carefully:.\n\nLet me write each digit position:.\n\nStarting from the right:.\n\n1. Ones place: 6 + 9 = 15 → 5, carryover 1.\n\n2. Tens place: 1 (from 800216) + 0 (from 359309) + 1 (carry) = 2. So tens digit is 2, no carryover.\n\n3. Hundreds place: 2 (from first number) + 3 (from second) = 5. So 5, no carry.\n\n4. Thousands place: 0 + 9 = 9. So 9, no carry.\n\n5. Ten-thousands place: 0 (from first number's ten-thousands place) + 5 (from second number's ten-thousands place, since 359309 is 3 (hundred thousands), 5 (ten thousands), etc.) → 0+5=5. So 5.\n\n6. Hundred-thousands place: 8 (from first number) + 3 (from second number) = 11. So here, we write down 1 in the hundred-thousands place and carry over 1 to the millions place.\n\nSince there's a carryover of 1 to the millions place, that becomes the next digit. So the total sum is 1 (million) 1 (hundred thousand) 5 (ten thousand) 9 (thousand) 5 (hundred) 2 (ten) 5 (one). So, let me directly conclude the answer. The answer is "]]
