ten thousands), 9 (thousands), 9 (hundreds), 3 (tens), 5 (ones).\n\nSecond number: 2 (hundred thousands), 1 (ten thousands), 3 (thousands), 3 (hundreds), 9 (tens), 2 (ones).\n\nAdding them column by column (right to left):\n\nOnes: 5+2=7.\n\nTens: 3+9=12 \u2192 2, carryover 1.\n\nHundreds:9+3=12 +1=13 \u2192 3, carryover 1.\n\nThousands:9+3=12 +1=13 \u2192 3, carryover 1.\n\nTen thousands:4+1=5 +1=6 \u2192 6, no carryover.\n\nHundred thousands:9+2=11 \u2192 11. Since there's no higher place, this is 11 in the hundred thousands place. But since 11 is more than one digit, we need to split it. Wait, actually, when adding, the hundred thousands column gives 11, so we write down 1 in the hundred thousands place and carry over 1 to the millions place. But since there was no millions place in the original numbers, the carryover creates a new digit at the millions place. So the total is:\n\nMillions place: 1 (from carryover).\n\nHundred thousands place: 1 (from 11 - 10).\n\nThen the rest as before.\n\nTherefore, putting it all together:\n\nThe digits from right to left (ones to millions):\n\nOnes:7\n\nTens:2\n\nHundreds:3\n\nThousands:3\n\nTen thousands:6\n\nHundred thousands:1 (with a carryover 1 to millions)\n\nMillions:1.\n\nSo the total number is 1 (million), 1 (hundred thousand), 6 (ten thousands), 3 (thousands), 3 (hundreds), 2 (tens), 7 (ones). Which is 1,163,327.\n\nWait, let me check with another method to be sure. Let's add the numbers directly:\n\n949,935 + 213,392.\n\nLet me break them into parts:\n\n949,935 + 200,000 = 1,149,935.\n\nThen add 13,392: 1,149,935 +13,392.\n\n1,149,935 +10,000 = 1,159,935.\n\nThen +3,392: 1,159,935 +3,000 = 1,162,935; then +392 gives 1,163,327. Okay, that matches.\n\nAlternatively, adding digit by digit again:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nFirst number: 949,935 \u2192 digits: 5,3,9,9,4,9.\n\nSecond number:213,392 \u2192 digits:2,9,3,3,1,2.\n\nWait, maybe writing them as:\n\n       9 4 9 9 3 5\n\n     + 2 1 3 3 9 2\n\n     ----------------\n\nStarting from the right (units):\n\n5+2=7.\n\n3+9=12 \u2192 2, carryover 1.\n\n9+3 +1=13 \u2192 3, carryover1.\n\n9+3 +1=13 \u21923, carryover1.\n\n4+1 +1=6 \u21926, no carryover.\n\n9+2=11 \u21921, carryover1.\n\nBut wait, the next digit (millions place) is the carryover 1. So the digits from right are:\n\n7,2,3,3,6,1 and then the carried over 1 at the beginning. So the number is 1,163,327. That's correct.\n\nTherefore, the final answer should be 1,163,327.\n\nWait, let me confirm once more with another method. Maybe approximate the numbers:\n\n949,935 is roughly 950,000, and 213,392 is roughly 213,000. Their sum is about 1,163,000. Which is close to the exact result of 1,163,327. So that seems right.\n\nSo the reasoning steps would be:\n\n1. Ones place: 5 + 2 = 7.\n\n2. Tens place: 3 + 9 = 12. Write 2, carryover 1.\n\n3. Hundreds place: 9 + 3 + 1 = 13. Write 3, carryover 1.\n\n4. Thousands place: 9 + 3 + 1 = 13. Write 3, carryover 1.\n\n5. Ten thousands place: 4 + 1 + 1 = 6. Write 6, no carryover.\n\n6. Hundred thousands place: 9 + 2 = 11. Write 1 here and carryover 1 to the millions place.\n\n7. Millions place: the carried over 1.\n\nPutting it all together gives 1,163,327.\n\nYes, that's correct.\n</think>\n\n",
