sands (3), thousands (3), hundreds (2), tens (5), ones (8).\n\nPutting it all together:\n\nMillions place:1,\n\nHundred thousands:2,\n\nTen thousands:3,\n\nThousands:3,\n\nHundreds:2,\n\nTens:5,\n\nOnes:8.\n\nSo the number is 1,233,258.\n\nWait, so the correct answer is 1,233,258. Let me verify this with another method to be sure.\n\nAlternatively, adding the numbers:\n\n332,460 + 900,798.\n\nLet me compute 332,460 + 900,000 first, which would be 1,232,460. Then add the remaining 798: 1,232,460 + 798 = 1,233,258. Yes, that matches. So the correct sum is 1,233,258.\n\nTherefore, my initial mistake was forgetting that the hundred thousands place addition would require a carryover into a new millions place. I need to ensure that when adding digits in any place, if the sum exceeds 9, we carry over to the next higher place, even if that place wasn't present in the original numbers.\n\nSo the step-by-step should include that carryover to the millions place. Let me adjust the reasoning accordingly.\n\nStarting over with the correct steps:\n\n1. Ones place: 0 +8=8.\n\n2. Tens place:6+9=15. Write 5, carryover 1.\n\n3. Hundreds place:4+7+1=12. Write 2, carryover 1.\n\n4. Thousands place:2+0+1=3. Write 3, no carryover.\n\n5. Ten thousands place:3+0=3. Write 3.\n\n6. Hundred thousands place:3+9=12. Write 2, carryover 1 to millions place.\n\n7. Millions place: The carried over 1 is written there.\n\nSo combining all digits from millions down: 1 (million), 2 (hundred thousand), 3 (ten thousand), 3 (thousand), 2 (hundred), 5 (ten), 8 (one). So 1,233,258.\n\nTherefore, the answer is correct now.\n"]
