ds): 2  .\nPosition 5 (ten thousands): 3  .\nPosition 6 (hundred thousands): 4  .\n\nSimilarly, 931239:.\n\nPosition 1 (ones): 9  .\nPosition 2 (tens): 3  .\nPosition 3 (hundreds): 2  .\nPosition 4 (thousands): 1  .\nPosition 5 (ten thousands): 3  .\nPosition 6 (hundred thousands): 9  .\n\nWait, maybe I should write them vertically properly:.\n\nLet me write each number digit by digit aligned properly:.\n\n          4 3 2 1 2 0.\n        + 9 3 1 2 3 9.\n        ----------------.\n\nWait, actually, when written vertically, the rightmost digits (ones place) should align. Let me confirm each digit's place:.\n\nStarting from the right:.\n\nFirst number: 432120.\n\nPositions (from right):.\n\n1: 0 (ones).\n2: 2 (tens).\n3: 1 (hundreds).\n4: 2 (thousands).\n5: 3 (ten thousands).\n6: 4 (hundred thousands).\n\nSecond number: 931239.\n\nPositions (from right):.\n\n1: 9 (ones).\n2: 3 (tens).\n3: 2 (hundreds).\n4: 1 (thousands).\n5: 3 (ten thousands).\n6: 9 (hundred thousands).\n\nSo when written vertically:.\n\n      4 3 2 1 2 0.\n    + 9 3 1 2 3 9.\n    ----------------.\n\nWait, actually, when adding, the digits should be aligned properly. Let me write them properly:.\n\nLet me write the first number as:.\n\nHundred thousands Ten thousands Thousands Hundreds Tens Ones.\n4                3           2       1     2     0.\n\nSecond number:.\n\n9                3           1       2     3     9.\n\nSo when adding, each column is:.\n\nOnes: 0 +9 =9.\n\nTens: 2 +3=5.\n\nHundreds:1 +2=3.\n\nThousands:2 +1=3.\n\nTen thousands:3 +3=6.\n\nHundred thousands:4 +9=13.\n\nWait, but that's only if there's no carry-over. Wait, but when adding each column, I might have to carry over if the sum exceeds 9. Let me check each column step by step.\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (Ones place):.\n\nOnes place: 0 + 9 = 9. Write down 9, no carry-over.\n\nTens place: 2 + 3 = 5. Write down 5, no carry-over.\n\nHundreds place: 1 + 2 = 3. Write down 3, no carry-over.\n\nThousands place: 2 + 1 = 3. Write down 3, no carry-over.\n\nTen thousands place: 3 + 3 = 6. Write down 6, no carry-over.\n\nHundred thousands place: 4 + 9 = 13. Hmm, here the sum is 13. Since this is the highest place here, we can just write 13 as part of the total. Wait, but actually, since it's a six-digit number, adding two six-digit numbers can result in a seven-digit number if the sum exceeds 999,999. So in this case, 4 (hundred thousands) plus 9 (hundred thousands) is 13 hundred thousands, which is 1,300,000. So the 13 in the hundred thousands place would mean that we have a 3 in the hundred thousands and a carry-over of 1 to a new million's place.\n\nWait a minute! I almost missed that. Since 4 +9 =13, which is two digits, so we have to carry over 1 to the millions place. So the sum would actually be a seven-digit number.\n\nLet me redo the addition step by step with proper carrying over.\n\nStarting from the right:.\n\n1. Ones place: 0 +9 =9. Write down 9. Carryover 0.\n\n2. Tens place: 2 +3 =5. Write down 5. Carryover 0.\n\n3. Hundreds place:1 +2=3. Write down 3. Carryover 0.\n\n4. Thousands place:2 +1=3. Write down 3. Carryover 0.\n\n5. Ten thousands place:3 +3=6. Write down 6. Carryover 0.\n\n6. Hundred thousands place:4 +9=13. Here, 13. So write down 3 in the hundred thousands place, and carryover 1 to the millions place.\n\nSince there are no more digits to add, the carryover 1 becomes the millions place. So the final number is 1 (million) 3 (hundred thousand) followed by the other digits.\n\nPutting it all together:.\n\nMillions place: 1.\n\nHundred thousands:3.\n\nTen thousands:6.\n\nThousands:3.\n\nHundreds:3.\n\nTens:5.\n\nOnes:9. So, let me directly conclude the answer. The answer is "]]
