ace (ten-thousands place).\n\nNext is the ten-thousands place. The first number has a 2, and the second has a 7. Adding the carried-over 1 gives 2 + 7 + 1 = 10. Again, that's 10. So we write 0 in the ten-thousands place and carry over 1 to the hundred-thousands place.\n\nFinally, the hundred-thousands place. The first number has a 1, and the second has a 9. Adding the carried-over 1 gives 1 + 9 + 1 = 11. So we write 1 in the hundred-thousands place and carry over 1 to the millions place. Since there are no more digits left, that carried-over 1 becomes the millions place digit.\n\nPutting it all together, the digits from right to left (starting from ones) are:.\n\nOnes: 7.\n\nTens: 8.\n\nHundreds: 0.\n\nThousands: 6.\n\nTen-thousands: 0.\n\nHundred-thousands: 1.\n\nMillions: 1.\n\nWait, let me check that again. Wait, when we added the hundred-thousands place (1 + 9 + 1), that's 11, so the hundred-thousands digit is 1 and carryover 1 to millions. So the millions digit is 1. So, let me directly conclude the answer. The answer is "]]
