ting all the digits together: 1 (hundred-thousands), 3 (ten-thousands?), Wait, wait a second. Let me check again. Wait, maybe I messed up the places. Let me recount the digits properly.\n\nWait, let's do this step by step again carefully.\n\nLet me write both numbers with their place values:.\n\n806,900 is:.\n\n- Hundred thousands: 8.\n- Ten thousands: 0.\n- Thousands: 6.\n- Hundreds: 9.\n- Tens: 0.\n- Ones: 0.\n\n553,981 is:.\n\n- Hundred thousands: 5.\n- Ten thousands: 5.\n- Thousands: 3.\n- Hundreds: 9.\n- Tens: 8.\n- Ones: 1.\n\nAdding each column starting from the right:.\n\nOnes place: 0 + 1 = 1 → write 1.\n\nTens place: 0 + 8 = 8 → write 8.\n\nHundreds place: 9 + 9 = 18 → write 8, carry over 1.\n\nThousands place: 6 + 3 = 9, plus the carried 1 makes 10 → write 0, carry over 1.\n\nTen thousands place: 0 + 5 = 5, plus the carried 1 → 6. Write 6, no carry.\n\nHundred thousands place: 8 + 5 = 13 → write 13.\n\nSo putting it all together: 1,3 (hundred thousands and ten thousands?), wait, let me structure this properly.\n\nWait, the hundred thousands place digits are 8 and 5. Their sum is 13. So that would be 13 in the hundred thousands place? But that's actually a 1 in the millions place and 3 in the hundred thousands?.\n\nWait a second, maybe I made a mistake here. Let me think again. When adding the hundred thousands place (the leftmost digits here):.\n\n8 (from 806,900) plus 5 (from 553,981) equals 13. Since there's no higher place beyond hundred thousands here, this 13 would actually represent 1,300,000. So the total sum would be 1,3 (million and hundred thousand?), but let me see the total sum step by step.\n\nWait, let me do the addition step by step again with the actual numbers:.\n\n806,900.\n\n+553,981.\n\nLet me add each place again step by step:.\n\nStarting from the right:.\n\nOnes: 0 +1 =1.\n\nTens:0 +8=8.\n\nHundreds:9+9=18 (write 8, carry 1).\n\nThousands:6 +3=9 plus the 1 is 10 (write 0, carry 1).\n\nTen thousands:0 +5=5 plus the 1 is 6 (write 6, no carry).\n\nHundred thousands:8 +5=13 (write 13).\n\nSo putting all digits together from right to left:.\n\nOnes:1, tens:8, hundreds:8, thousands:0, ten thousands:6, hundred thousands:3, and the carried over 1 in the millions place. Wait, that's the key point I might have messed up before. The 13 in the hundred thousands place actually creates a new digit in the millions place.\n\nWait, the hundred thousands place can only hold a single digit. So when adding 8 and 5, which gives 13, that's 10 hundred thousands plus 3 hundred thousands. 10 hundred thousands is equal to 1 million. So that's why we need to carry over the 1 to the millions place.\n\nTherefore, the sum would be:.\n\nMillions place: 1.\n\nHundred thousands place: 3.\n\nTen thousands place: 6.\n\nThousands place:0.\n\nHundreds place:8.\n\nTens place:8.\n\nOnes place:1. So, let me directly conclude the answer. The answer is "]]
