6 =1,187,906. Yes, that's the same. So the answer is correct.\n\nSo the step-by-step reasoning should correctly reflect that when adding the hundred thousands digits, there's a carryover to the millions place. So in the reasoning steps, after the hundred thousands place addition, we need to mention that carryover. Let me structure that properly in the template.\n\nSo the digits in order from right (ones) to left (hundred thousands):\n\nOnes:6+0=6\n\nTens:5+5=10 (write 0, carry 1)\n\nHundreds:2+6=8 +1=9\n\nThousands:6+1=7\n\nTen-thousands:1+7=8\n\nHundred-thousands:9+2=11 (write 1, carry 1 to millions place)\n\nThen the carried 1 is added as the millions digit. So the final number is 1 (million) 1 (hundred thousand) 8 (ten thousand) 7 (thousand) 9 (hundred) 0 (ten) 6 (one). So 1,187,906.\n\nTherefore, the reasoning steps should detail each digit addition, including the carryovers, especially the final carryover to the millions place.\n"]
