housands place is 0 (from 8+1 +1=10, so 0 with carryover). \n\nHundreds place is 1.\n\nTens place 3, ones 2.\n\nSo combining all digits: 1,2 (million?), wait, no. Wait, 12 in the hundred thousands place would mean 1,200,000. Then the ten thousands is 0, thousands 0, hundreds 1, tens 3, ones 2. So total is 1,200,132. \n\nWait, but 12 in the hundred thousands place is actually 1.2 million? Because 100,000 *12 is 1,200,000. So yes. Then the rest is 0 in ten thousands, 0 in thousands, 1 in hundreds, 3 in tens, 2 in ones. So altogether 1,200,132. \n\nLet me check with another method, maybe breaking the numbers into parts:\n\n698,259 + 501,873.\n\nLet's split them:\n\n698,259 = 600,000 + 98,259\n\n501,873 = 500,000 + 1,873\n\nAdding the large parts: 600,000 + 500,000 = 1,100,000\n\nThen the smaller parts: 98,259 +1,873.\n\n98,259 +1,873:\n\n98,259 + 1,000 = 99,259\n\n99,259 + 873 = 100,132.\n\nSo total is 1,100,000 + 100,132 = 1,200,132. Okay that matches. So the answer should be 1,200,132. \n\nI think that's correct. Let me just add the numbers straight again:\n\n698259\n\n+501873\n\nAdd the ones:9+3=12. 2, carryover 1.\n\nTens:5+7=12 +1=13. 3, carryover1.\n\nHundreds:2+8=10+1=11. 1, carryover1.\n\nThousands:8+1=9 +1=10. 0, carryover1.\n\nTen thousands:9+0=9 +1=10. 0, carryover1.\n\nHundred thousands:6+5=11 +1=12. So writing 12 here. So the total digits from right to left are 2,3,1,0,0,2,1 (wait, but how does that form the number? Let me see:\n\nThe places are:\n\nOnes:2\n\nTens:3\n\nHundreds:1\n\nThousands:0\n\nTen thousands:0\n\nHundred thousands:2 (from 12?), but actually the 12 in the hundred thousands place would be represented as 2 in hundred thousands and carryover to millions. Wait, I think I made a mistake here.\n\nWait, when adding the hundred thousands place, which is the 6 (from first number) and 5 (from second number), plus the carryover 1 from the previous step (ten thousands place), so 6 +5 +1 =12. Since this is the hundred thousands place, 12 here would m