o 2 in thousands place, carryover 1.\n\nTen thousands:6 +6 =12, plus carryover 1 makes 13. So 3 in ten thousands, carryover 1.\n\nHundred thousands:7 +4 =11, plus carryover 1 makes 12. So 2 in hundred thousands, carryover 1.\n\nThen the carryover 1 is the millions place.\n\nSo putting together: 1 (million), 2 (hundred thousand), 3 (ten thousand), 2 (thousand), 7 (hundred),9 (ten),9 (one). So that's 1,232,799? Wait, wait, no. Wait the thousands digit was 2, but the hundreds is 7, then the tens 9, ones 9. Wait, the thousands place is 2, but the ten thousands is 3, hundred thousands 2, and million 1. So the number is 1,232,799? Wait, no, let me write it step by step.\n\nLet me list each digit's place:\n\nMillions: 1\n\nHundred thousands:2\n\nTen thousands:3\n\nThousands:2\n\nHundreds:7\n\nTens:9\n\nOnes:9\n\nSo combining them: 1,232,799. Wait, so the first method had an error in the thousands place?\n\nWait, in the first calculation I thought the thousands place was 7+4=11 plus carryover 1, making 12. So thousands digit 2, carryover 1. That's correct. So the thousands place is 2, so the thousands digit is 2, so in the number it's ...2,799. Wait, but the hundreds place is 7. So 2 (thousands), 7 (hundreds), so the number is ...2799.\n\nWait, so that gives 1,232,799. But earlier when I thought I had 1,232,809, that must have been a miscalculation. Let me check the alternative method again:\n\n767,914 +464,885.\n\nAdding 767,914 +464,885:\n\nLet me add the numbers digit by digit:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nOnes:4+5=9.\n\nTens:1+8=9.\n\nHundreds:9+8=17 (write 7, carryover 1).\n\nThousands:7 (from 767,914's thousands place is 7 in the thousands place? Wait, let's see:\n\nWait the first number is 767,914. Breaking down:\n\nDigits: 7 (hundred thousands), 6 (ten thousands), 7 (thousands), 9 (hundreds), 1 (tens), 4 (ones).\n\nSecond number:464,885 is 4 (hundred thousands), 6 (ten thousands),4 (thousands),8 (hundreds),8 (tens),5 (ones).\n\nSo thousands place for first number 