rom the right:.\n\nOnes place: 3 + 0 = 3. .\n\nTens place: 9 + 9 = 18. Write 8, carryover 1.\n\nHundreds place: 7 (from first number) + 1 (from second number) = 8, plus the carried 1 makes 9. So 9 here, no carryover.\n\nThousands place: 4 (from first) +7 (from second) = 11. So write 1, carryover 1.\n\nTen-thousands place: 9 (from first number) + 4 (from second number) = 13, plus the carried 1 is 14. So write 4, carryover 1.\n\nHundred-thousands place: 1 (from first number) +9 (from second number) = 10, plus the carried 1 is 11. So here we have 11. Since this is the highest place, we just write both digits, so 1 and 1, making the total so far 1 1 followed by the previous digits?.\n\nWait, putting it all together:.\n\nThe digits from right to left (starting from ones):.\n\nOnes:3, Tens:8, Hundreds:9, Thousands:1 (with a carryover), Ten-thousands:4 (with carryover), Hundred-thousands: 11 (plus carryover). .\n\nWait, so when writing the total sum, the hundred-thousands place would be 11, so that's 1,1 followed by the other digits. .\n\nPutting all digits together from left to right:.\n\nThe highest place is the hundred-thousands. Since the first number is 194,793 (so 1 in hundred thousands) and the second is 947,190 (9 in hundred thousands), adding 1+9 gives 10, plus the carried over 1 makes 11. So that's 1,1 in the hundred thousands and ten thousands place? Wait, maybe I need to think of each digit's place again.\n\nLet me try writing the addition vertically with each digit aligned properly:.\n\n        1 9 4 7 9 3.\n      + 9 4 7 1 9 0.\n      ----------------.\nStarting from the right (ones):.\n\n3 + 0 = 3 (ones place).\n\nNext (tens): 9 + 9 = 18. Write 8, carryover 1.\n\nNext (hundreds): 7 + 1 = 8, plus 1 carried over is 9 (hundreds place).\n\nNext (thousands): 4 + 7 = 11. Write 1, carryover 1.\n\nNext (ten thousands): 9 + 4 = 13, plus 1 carried over is 14. So write 4 here, carryover 1.\n\nNext (hundred thousands): 1 + 9 = 10, plus the carried over 1 is 11. So here we have 11 in the hundred thousands place. Since each digit is a separate place, 11 in the hundred thousands would actually be 1,100,000? Wait, no. Wait, the hundred thousands place can only hold a single digit. So if it's 11, that means we need to carry over to the millions place. Oh! Right, I forgot that adding the hundred thousands could result in a million. .\n\nAh, here's a mistake. The hundred thousands place digits are 1 and 9, so 1 +9=10, plus the carried 1 from the previous step (which was the ten thousands place) gives 11. So actually, the hundred thousands place can't hold 11. So 11 in the hundred thousands place is 1 million and 1 hundred thousand. Therefore, we need to carry over 1 to the millions place. .\n\nSo the total digits would now have a millions place. Let me recast that:.\n\nStarting over, with proper carrying:.\n\nLet me write each step again with attention to the carryover beyond hundred thousands:.\n\nStarting from the right:.\n\nOnes: 3+0=3.\n\nTens:9+9=18 → 8, carryover 1.\n\nHundreds:7+1+1=9 (since carryover from tens is 1).\n\nThousands:4+7=11 → 1, carryover 1.\n\nTen thousands:9+4 +1 (carryover) =14 → 4, carryover 1.\n\nHundred thousands:1+9=10 +1 (carryover) =11 → here, 11, so this is 1 (million) and 1 (hundred thousand). So we need to write 1 in the hundred thousands place and carryover 1 to the millions place.\n\nSo the millions place now has the carried over 1, so the total is 1 (million), 1 (hundred thousand), 4 (ten thousand), 1 (thousand), 9 (hundred), 8 (ten), 3 (one). .\n\nSo putting it all together:. So, let me directly conclude the answer. The answer is "]] 86%|########5 | 428/500 [4:31:15<38:52, 32.40s/it]
