ng those gives 9 + 3 = 12. Since there's no more columns to carry over, that's just 12. But wait, in a six-digit number, the hundred thousands place can only hold a single digit. Oh, right, but since the total is 12 here, that means it's actually 12 in the hundred thousands place? Wait, no, that's not right. Wait, let me check again.\n\nWait, maybe I messed up the place values. Let me recount the digits. The first number is 906,425 which is 9 (hundred thousands), 0 (ten thousands), 6 (thousands), 4 (hundreds), 2 (tens), 5 (ones). The second number is 319,094: 3 (hundred thousands), 1 (ten thousands), 9 (thousands), 0 (hundreds), 9 (tens), 4 (ones). So when adding the hundred thousands place: 9 + 3 = 12. So that's correct. Since there's no higher place, that becomes 12 in the hundred thousands and ten thousands? Wait, actually, the hundred thousands place can only have one digit. So 12 in the hundred thousands place would mean we have to carry over to a new digit. Wait a second, so adding 900,000 and 300,000 gives 1,200,000. So that's 12 in the hundred thousands place, which is actually 1 million and 2 hundred thousands. So, let me directly conclude the answer. The answer is "]]
